number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|An unapproved administrator controls a synchronization server|A principal outside the approved control-plane boundary has effective local administrative control of a confirmed synchronization host connected to the tenant.|Host identity; effective local administrators; service and connector configuration metadata; tenant linkage; approved administrator inventory.|Move synchronization hosts into the protected administrative boundary and remove the unintended host administrator.|A host name containing AAD or SYNC is not discovery evidence. Host control does not imply every cloud-native administrator can be taken over.|T1556.007|AC-3;AC-6;SC-7|Enforce the administrative boundary around infrastructure that influences hybrid authentication.||High
002|A writable GPO reaches a synchronization server|An unapproved principal can modify a GPO AND resultant policy confirms an execution or local-admin setting from that GPO applies to a synchronization host.|GPO AD ACL and SYSVOL ACL; links; inheritance; filtering; resultant policy; setting semantics; host-to-tenant relationship.|Restrict GPO and SYSVOL writes and use a dedicated policy boundary for synchronization hosts.|A linked GPO is not necessarily applied. Read-only GPO rights, WMI filtering, or a disabled relevant half can break the path.|T1484.001;T1556.007|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Protect effective configuration paths that can change a synchronization host's administrative state.|MDI-GPO|High
003|A delegated reset reaches a replication-capable connector account|An unapproved principal can reset a confirmed connector account AND that account has the effective domain replication rights needed for secret replication.|Connector-to-account binding; account ACL; all required replication rights at domain root; account status; intended feature permissions.|Restrict connector-account control and reduce replication permissions to the supported minimum.|Do not perform DCSync. Required replication rights depend on the operation; one similarly named ACE is not sufficient.|T1098;T1003.006|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Protect the connector authenticator and tightly limit directory replication privileges.|MDI-HYBRID|High
004|A lower-trust operator can change active synchronization rules|A principal can change active synchronization rules or connector configuration AND the permitted changes affect identities or groups with sensitive cloud grants.|Configuration administration rights; supported rule export; source and target scopes; current grants; change records.|Restrict synchronization configuration changes and review the objects those rules can affect.|Do not infer unrestricted cloud takeover. Existing-source updates and new hard matches have different protections.|T1556.007;T1484|AC-3;CM-3;CM-5|Control changes to identity authority and review their downstream authorization effects.|MS-MATCH-2026|High
005|A Cloud Sync service identity is retrievable outside its intended boundary|A principal has effective authorization to retrieve a Cloud Sync gMSA secret AND the gMSA is bound to a registered provisioning agent with sensitive directory permissions.|gMSA retrieval ACL and group expansion; agent binding; service identity; effective directory rights; host inventory.|Limit gMSA retrieval authorization to the required protected agent hosts.|Inspect authorization metadata only. Do not request msDS-ManagedPassword; group membership without effective retrieval access is not sufficient.|T1078.002;T1556.007|AC-6;IA-5|Limit access to service authenticators that support cross-directory provisioning.|MS-GMSA|High
006|An abandoned connector account retains a hybrid control path|A connector account has no approved active or staging deployment after owner review AND remains enabled with replication, writeback, or privileged cloud permissions.|Deployment inventory; owner attestation; last observed use with coverage; effective AD and cloud grants; account state.|Retire the unused connector identity through change control and remove its remaining grants.|No recent sign-in by itself does not establish abandonment. Staging, disaster recovery, and intermittent use must be checked.|T1078.002;T1078.004|AC-2;AC-6|Remove unneeded identities and privileges after integration retirement.||High
007|Writeback permissions reach privileged AD targets|A deployed writeback service has effective reset or write authority over protected or other control-plane AD accounts beyond the approved supported target scope.|Writeback mode; service identity; effective target ACLs; target classification; platform-supported restrictions; target scope.|Restrict writeback delegation and exclude unsupported or privileged targets through supported configuration.|Cloud-side password reset is not automatically an AD reset. Protected accounts and supported-operation restrictions can block the operation.|T1098;T1556.007|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Limit the direction and scope of cross-boundary authenticator changes.|MS-WRITEBACK|High
008|A staging synchronization host sits outside the protected boundary|A staging or standby host retains connector configuration or privileged service identities AND lower-trust administrators can control it.|Staging status; retained service identities and grants; host administrator paths; planned activation procedure.|Protect standby hosts to the same boundary as active synchronization infrastructure or securely retire them.|Staging status means no current exports, not no sensitive material. Report retained privilege rather than inventing active synchronization.|T1556.007;T1078.002|AC-6;CM-8;IA-5|Inventory dormant control-plane assets and protect the privileges retained for recovery.||High
